on we defined earlier  the one that takes a 2d Point as a seed  value that is to say it takes two values  and munches them together somehow  spitting back a pseudo-random number  using this hash function at each of  these points on the grid we'd have a  random value let's represent those with  grayscale points and that means that at  any given point in 2D space you find the  four surrounding points of the grid with  those you can perform bilinear  interpolation to get another value and  voila you have your noise value what  we've just created this is what's called  value noise the reason is called value  noise is simple at each point in our  grid we have a random value and those  are interpolated to form noise values  that's all no deeper meaning than that  the only problem is this whole thing  looks pretty blocky there's a pretty  obvious grid pattern here now if you  watch the video on lerp you should feel  comfortable swapping in smooth step or  another variant like smoother step which  gives you immediately better results and  if you didn't watch the video shame on  you go watch it now this is an  improvement but there's another even  better way to get smoother looking noise  though it's called gradient noise and  the idea is similar to Value noise  you've got your grid but instead of  having a grid of points we have a grid  of gradients or basically random  Direction vectors when you sample a  point you find the four closest  gradients and this is where things get  slightly different for each of those  four points you calculate the direction  to the points take the dot product  between the gradient at Each corner and  the direction to that corner and then  you interpolate between those four  values and what you end up with looks  like this which to me looks a lot better  than value noise at the cost of being  more expensive to calculate now we've  mostly touched on value and gradient  noise but before moving on there's  another form of noise that's also pretty  popular and versatile more annoying  noise the idea is super simple you start  with a basic grid kind of like we've  already been doing at every cell in the  grid you start with a point in the  center and then offset it by some amount  using a hash function then to calculate  the noise for any given position figure  out which cell you're in and which cells  are adjacent Loop through them all and  take the Min of the distance between the  position and the cell's offset point and  that ends up giving you something that  looks like this it it's a little bit  organic looking and can be really useful  for any situation where you want a kind  of cellular pattern now you have the  basis for something very cool so watch  this  so here's the plot of an ordinary sine  wave what happens when you take a sine  wave and you add a second one on top  nothing special at first the Peaks and  troughs just get higher and lower but if  we change the second make it higher  frequency but lower amplitude now once  we overlay that it adds a bit of detail  a bit of extra complexity and if I do  that again we'll overlay yet another  sine wave even higher frequency but  lower amplitude and the complexity  increases and if we do this again and  again we end up with these incredibly  complex patterns depending on how we  change the frequency and amplitude of  course  and if I take noise and do the exact  same thing that is overlay multiple  noises at different frequencies and  amplitudes you start to see some really  cool details emerging from this  seemingly random suit this is called  fractional Brownian motion or fbm and it  might not be apparent just how powerful  a tool this noise is it can be useful  for anything you can do things like  adding a tiny bit of detail to a  material to displacing and contouring  the surface itself  modulating the intensity of a light for  a flickering effect or adding a super  easy to do dramatic camera shake with a  single line of code  let's do some more in-depth examples the  classic example is terrain if we look at  the ground at every 2D point we can  sample fractional Brownian motion for  the height to bring it to life giving  you pretty much infinite procedural 3D  worlds and the noise itself can be run  through shaping functions to give it  more personality if I were to say run  ABS on the first layer of noise and then  add a bunch of much more high frequency  noise I get a more desert-like scene  where I can increase the contribution of  the other layers of noise and scale the  whole thing a bit giving me Rocky  mountainous terrain and I can even  flatten out the lower lying areas by  applying a Min value and then coloring  that quickly to give us water I mean at  this point it's basically open-ended you  can do whatever you want by shaping the  functions of it panning out a bit let's  add some random stars because why not  and these are generated on the grid  using hash functions and if you look  closely the big ones twinkle and that's  achieved by Sam noise over time and we  can bring in a planet now by sampling  the fbm using 3D coordinates you get  this noisy looking surface if I  interpret the grayscale values a bit  black being lower and white being higher  some greens and blues for oceans and  land some deserts some ice caps and  we've got ourselves a decent little  world for a couple minutes of work and  this has infinite possibilities  obviously we can add time as a fourth  dimension to the noise  and as I scroll the noise through time  the continents move and shift  effortlessly interpreting the noise in  different ways gives different effects  by blending between successive rotating  bands of noise then using a trick that  is sampling the noise again using the  noise as an offset otherwise known as  domain warping we can build other kinds  of planets it's limited by your  imagination or maybe planets aren't your  bag so we can do something cool let's  start with the base color and we'll use  more annoy noise to build distinct  little cells we'll sample the fbm using  the direction from the cell center to  streak a bit and then kind of just shade  the top darker than the bottom to fake  some lighting we'll draw a circle at the  center of each cell color it white and  then add another circle inside with a  random color and some streaks it might  be getting obvious where I'm going with  this now we can use smooth step to  darken the cell around the eye and also  darken generally the top right portion  making this look deeper a classic  marbling pattern with some red and blue  can create some convincing veins and  some lighting using the circles normal  and a fake highlight using smooth step  gives you some shading finally we do a  step over time to mask the eye from top  to bottom and we've got ourselves a  creepy little effect here feels a bit  Halloweeny  like always I'll get the code up on  GitHub at some point in the meantime you  should check out my Shader course for a  much longer step-by-step walkthrough of  these Concepts along with others  and if you have ideas and suggestions  for future videos make sure to comment  and respond also make sure to subscribe  cheers
 

